'''''Juncus maritimus''''', known as the '''sea rush''', is a species of [[Juncaceae|rush]] that grows on coastlines.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://plants.usda.gov/java/profile?symbol=JUMA5|title=USDA Plants Database}}</ref> It is sometimes considered [[conspecific]] with ''[[Juncus kraussii]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://florabase.dpaw.wa.gov.au/search/current?id=8778 |title=FloraBase Name Currency Search: Juncus maritimus Lam |access-date=2013-07-11 |archive-date=2013-07-11 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130711040252/http://florabase.dpaw.wa.gov.au/search/current?id=8778 |url-status=dead }}</ref>
It has a wide distribution across the western [[Palearctic realm]] (all of Europe, western Asia and the [[Maghreb]]).<ref>{{cite web |title=Juncus maritimus Lam. |url=https://www.catalogueoflife.org/data/taxon/3QTWD |publisher=[[Catalogue of Life]] |access-date=12 March 2021}}</ref><ref>Paul Adam {{Google books|BokNrZxqlr4C|Saltmarsh Ecology|page=204}}</ref>

According to [[Edward Catich]] the ancient Egyptians used ''Juncus maritimus'' as a brush for writing. He describes the process of making one: “the end of which [the rush] was cut at a slant and its fibers split by chewing to produce a small chisel-shaped ‘brush‘.”.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Catich |first1=Edward |title=The Origin of the Serif |isbn=0-9629740-1-3 |page=98}}</ref>

